<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Raised transferrin saturation and ferritin with raised liver enzymes in a 55-year-old man with severe alcohol use disorder. Please advise on the cause, whether and how to stage his liver, and treatment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, on the same day as his two-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the HFE test shows hemochromatosis, how much of the iron overload is genetic and how much is due to alcohol?</item><item>Does his liver need staging, and can this be done without a biopsy?</item><item>What phlebotomy schedule and ferritin target do you advise?</item><item>Is naltrexone safe for his liver, and what should we tell him about alcohol?</item><item>Does he need liver cancer surveillance, and what should his relatives and his diet do?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Hemingway, a 55-year-old writer, is seen at the request of Dr. Brandt-Ochoa. He has drunk heavily for many years and has cut down from about ten drinks a day to five or six in the two weeks since he started naltrexone. A year ago, in two plane crashes, he had a concussion with a skull fracture and injuries to his right kidney, liver and spleen; he was not told of any lasting liver damage. He has no jaundice, abdominal swelling, bleeding, confusion or joint pain in his hands. He takes no iron, vitamin C or other supplements and eats raw oysters now and then. He has not been told of hemochromatosis in his family. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ernest Hemingway was born on July 21, 1899, in Oak Park, Illinois, the son of a physician. [D]</item><item>In the First World War he joined a volunteer ambulance unit in the Italian army; in 1918 he was wounded at the front near Fossalta di Piave by a mortar shell, was decorated by the Italian government, and spent a long time in hospitals. [D]</item><item>He drank heavily for most of his adult life. [D]</item><item>In January 1954, on a safari in Africa, he survived two plane crashes on consecutive days in Uganda. In the second he forced a jammed door open with his head and suffered a concussion and a skull fracture; he also reported a ruptured right kidney and injuries to his liver and spleen. The Nobel Prize biography records that the accidents caused severe pain for the rest of his life. [D]</item><item>He was awarded the 1954 Nobel Prize in Literature and could not attend the ceremony in Stockholm because of ill health. [D]</item><item>His home from 1939 to 1960 was Finca Vigía, a farm on a hillside outside Havana, Cuba, where he wrote much of his work. [D]</item><item>His father, a physician who was described as depressed and diabetic, died by suicide in 1928. [D]</item><item>Several earlier members of his family also had depression and died by suicide, and he understood himself to be descended from men who had died by suicide on both sides of his family. [D]</item></list><paragraph>8 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

